1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:06,378
Phone number verification asks an outside service whether a contact's number is real,

2
00:00:06,378 --> 00:00:08,387
and what kind of line it is.

3
00:00:08,387 --> 00:00:12,347
This video shows why it exists, how to set up a provider, how

4
00:00:12,347 --> 00:00:16,070
to read the results, and how to work the queue of checks.

5
00:00:20,670 --> 00:00:24,336
A purchased list, an old import or a form typed in a hurry

6
00:00:24,336 --> 00:00:26,742
is full of numbers that do not work.

7
00:00:26,742 --> 00:00:30,580
Every call to one of them is time an agent does not get back.

8
00:00:31,330 --> 00:00:33,430
The kind of line matters too.

9
00:00:33,430 --> 00:00:37,390
A text sent to a landline is never read, and a mobile number

10
00:00:37,390 --> 00:00:39,730
is the one most likely to answer.

11
00:00:40,480 --> 00:00:45,347
Checking each number by hand is not realistic, and the services that do

12
00:00:45,347 --> 00:00:47,370
it charge for every check.

13
00:00:48,120 --> 00:00:54,837
Verification checks each number once, automatically, and shows the answer next to the number:

14
00:00:54,837 --> 00:00:57,890
valid or not, mobile, landline or VoIP.

15
00:01:02,090 --> 00:01:06,955
A number is checked when a contact is saved with a new or changed number.

16
00:01:06,955 --> 00:01:10,218
The site checks the contact's preferred number:

17
00:01:10,218 --> 00:01:13,420
cell first, then home, office, work and other.

18
00:01:14,170 --> 00:01:19,091
A verified number is checked again after the revalidation interval, so a number

19
00:01:19,091 --> 00:01:22,275
that stopped working does not stay green forever.

20
00:01:22,275 --> 00:01:25,980
A background task looks for due numbers every few minutes.

21
00:01:26,730 --> 00:01:29,857
Someone can also retry a check from the queue.

22
00:01:29,857 --> 00:01:35,390
Because the services charge per check, a number is never checked more often than this.

23
00:01:36,140 --> 00:01:39,779
If no provider is set up yet, new numbers wait, and they are

24
00:01:39,779 --> 00:01:42,480
checked as soon as a provider is available.

25
00:01:46,754 --> 00:01:49,858
Open Settings, Phone Number Verifications.

26
00:01:49,858 --> 00:01:54,190
The General tab holds the rules that apply to every provider.

27
00:01:54,190 --> 00:01:58,974
Until a provider is switched on, the page says that none is enabled.

28
00:01:59,471 --> 00:02:01,890
Each service has its own tab.

29
00:02:01,890 --> 00:02:05,901
AbstractAPI needs only an API key from your account.

30
00:02:09,839 --> 00:02:12,728
Twilio asks for an Authentication type:

31
00:02:12,728 --> 00:02:17,673
an API key SID and secret, which Twilio recommends for everyday use, or

32
00:02:17,673 --> 00:02:21,333
an Account SID and Auth Token, which suits testing.

33
00:02:21,333 --> 00:02:26,021
You can also set a Country code for numbers stored without one, and

34
00:02:26,021 --> 00:02:29,039
Data packages for extra line information.

35
00:02:35,820 --> 00:02:41,289
On the Veriphone tab, switch on Enable this provider and paste the API key.

36
00:02:41,289 --> 00:02:45,880
The key is stored securely, and the box stays empty afterward.

37
00:02:47,773 --> 00:02:48,848
Click Save.

38
00:02:48,848 --> 00:02:52,134
The General tab now offers a Default provider.

39
00:02:52,134 --> 00:02:58,073
Choose a provider, or First available provider to use the first one that is switched on.

40
00:02:58,532 --> 00:03:03,568
Revalidation interval says how many days a verified number stays verified.

41
00:03:03,568 --> 00:03:08,541
Maximum verification attempts is how many failed checks in a row the site

42
00:03:08,541 --> 00:03:12,192
accepts before it stops retrying a number by itself.

43
00:03:12,647 --> 00:03:17,608
Request delay is the pause between checks when many numbers are checked in a row.

44
00:03:17,608 --> 00:03:21,257
Raise it if the service complains about too many requests.

45
00:03:21,257 --> 00:03:22,227
Click Save.

46
00:03:26,527 --> 00:03:31,131
Wherever a phone field shows a checked number, an icon shows the result.

47
00:03:31,131 --> 00:03:32,370
Open a contact:

48
00:03:32,370 --> 00:03:35,853
a green check means the service confirmed the number.

49
00:03:35,853 --> 00:03:38,627
Point at it to see the date of the check.

50
00:03:39,280 --> 00:03:41,149
A red icon means Invalid:

51
00:03:41,149 --> 00:03:43,983
the service says the number is not valid.

52
00:03:43,983 --> 00:03:47,420
Do not call it, and ask the customer for a new one.

53
00:03:47,909 --> 00:03:50,176
A warning sign means Failed:

54
00:03:50,176 --> 00:03:55,846
the check itself did not work, for example because the service was unavailable.

55
00:03:55,846 --> 00:04:01,849
The status is unknown, not invalid, and a failed check never marks a number invalid.

56
00:04:02,398 --> 00:04:06,126
A grey question mark means the number has not been checked yet.

57
00:04:06,126 --> 00:04:09,098
It is checked shortly after the contact is saved.

58
00:04:13,398 --> 00:04:16,154
Open Tools, Phone Verifications Queue.

59
00:04:16,154 --> 00:04:20,915
It lists every number that has verification data, with a tile for each

60
00:04:20,915 --> 00:04:23,358
status that adds up to the total.

61
00:04:23,928 --> 00:04:28,434
Each row shows the number, its status, the provider, how many attempts were

62
00:04:28,434 --> 00:04:32,328
made, and when the last one was, with the contact it belongs to.

63
00:04:32,818 --> 00:04:34,576
Click the Failed tile.

64
00:04:34,576 --> 00:04:39,538
Failed means the last check failed and the site will try again by itself.

65
00:04:39,994 --> 00:04:45,100
Needs attention means the check failed Maximum verification attempts times in a row,

66
00:04:45,100 --> 00:04:46,972
so the site stopped trying.

67
00:04:46,972 --> 00:04:50,716
The latest error is shown in red, so you can tell a provider

68
00:04:50,716 --> 00:04:52,474
outage from a bad number.

69
00:04:53,038 --> 00:04:56,783
Use Search by phone number to find a record, and the sort list

70
00:04:56,783 --> 00:04:59,978
to put the most recent or the oldest attempts first.

71
00:05:02,554 --> 00:05:07,340
To check one number again, open its Actions menu and click Verify again.

72
00:05:07,340 --> 00:05:10,591
A failed number has a Retry now button instead.

73
00:05:10,591 --> 00:05:13,597
The check is queued and runs shortly after.

74
00:05:13,597 --> 00:05:18,014
Here the service cannot be reached, so the record shows the error.

75
00:05:18,583 --> 00:05:22,018
Tick several records, then click Retry selected.

76
00:05:22,018 --> 00:05:29,143
Retry all failed re-queues every failed and needs-attention record on every page that matches your search.

77
00:05:33,396 --> 00:05:37,002
Open Reports, General, Phone Number Verifications.

78
00:05:37,002 --> 00:05:43,443
Verification status counts the contacts, and the verified, unverified and invalid numbers, the

79
00:05:43,443 --> 00:05:45,826
failures, and the success rate.

80
00:05:46,440 --> 00:05:51,174
Line and queue status shows how many numbers are mobile, landline or VoIP,

81
00:05:51,174 --> 00:05:55,080
how many are pending, and how many need to be checked again.

82
00:05:55,661 --> 00:06:00,964
Provider usage shows how many records each provider checked, which is useful when

83
00:06:00,964 --> 00:06:03,464
you compare what the services cost.

84
00:06:03,464 --> 00:06:07,061
The Export CSV button downloads the report as a file.

85
00:06:11,261 --> 00:06:15,685
Before a calling campaign, import the list and let the checks run.

86
00:06:15,685 --> 00:06:20,601
Agents then work the verified numbers, and the invalid ones are set aside.

87
00:06:21,351 --> 00:06:24,993
Before a text campaign, check how many numbers are mobile.

88
00:06:24,993 --> 00:06:27,611
Those are the ones that can read a text.

89
00:06:28,361 --> 00:06:30,567
Keep the data clean over time.

90
00:06:30,567 --> 00:06:34,981
The revalidation interval brings old numbers back for a new check.

91
00:06:35,731 --> 00:06:38,000
Watch the Needs attention tile.

92
00:06:38,000 --> 00:06:42,661
A sudden jump usually means the provider key expired or the service is

93
00:06:42,661 --> 00:06:46,771
down, and a single Retry all failed fixes it once it is back.

94
00:06:48,771 --> 00:06:53,154
Set up a provider once, and every new number is checked for you.

95
00:06:53,154 --> 00:06:58,352
Read the icon next to the number, work the queue, and keep the results fresh.

96
00:06:58,352 --> 00:06:59,981
Thanks for watching.

